WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.013 --> 00:00:12.180
JONATHAN KELLER: Thank you
all for joining me today.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.555
My name is Jonathan
Keller, and I

00:00:13.555 --> 00:00:15.628
have the privilege
of being the Director

00:00:15.628 --> 00:00:17.420
of Product Management
for the BigQuery team

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:18.560
here at Google Cloud.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.327
Very excited to
have the opportunity

00:00:20.327 --> 00:00:22.160
to walk you through a
lot of the innovations

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:24.260
that we're working on
to help you unlock value

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:26.080
from your data.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.670
It's no secret why
we're all here.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:29.500
The world has become
much more data-driven,

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.000
and every company
and every industry

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.550
is on their journey to figure
out how to leverage data

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:34.900
to drive value.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.400
Unfortunately though,
many companies

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.130
are still struggling to
realize tangible and measurable

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:41.200
value from their
data, but they want

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.450
to learn how to provide
better customer experiences

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:45.340
or drive operational
excellence through more

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:48.563
intelligent decision making
and improve processes.

00:00:48.563 --> 00:00:50.980
Happy to be here to share with
you many of the innovations

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.870
that BigQuery is working on to
help customers around the world

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:57.170
finally be able to untap
the power of their data.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:58.730
If you're not familiar
with BigQuery,

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:02.390
it is Google's cloud-scale
enterprise data warehouse.

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:04.580
It sits at the center of
our open platform strategy

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:06.750
that delivers value from data.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.270
And of course,
standard SQL with DML.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.850
It's truly serverless,
multitenant, encrypted,

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:15.540
durable, and ready for the
largest internet scale services

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.580
and also highly secure
enterprise workloads

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.710
from gigabytes to exabytes.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.650
It is also intelligent, with
built in ML, extensibility,

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.570
and support for
in-memory analytics.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.280
It has a simplified
architecture that

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.770
unifies real time
and batch workloads

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:32.140
and supports virtually unlimited
number of analytics use cases.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:34.710
And it does this all with
industry-leading reliability

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.245
and predictable costs.

00:01:37.245 --> 00:01:39.120
Just to drill into a
little bit of how Google

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.280
is able to uniquely
deliver this value,

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.740
BigQuery takes advantage of
Google's full stack innovations

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.930
from hardware and
storages, network,

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.580
all the way up to
the software that

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:52.230
has been used to analyze data
for search and advertising

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:53.470
for over a decade.

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:55.980
We are not strangers
to big data analytics.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:57.690
This full stack
approach allows you

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:59.550
to access stateless
compute, which

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:02.340
enables virtually unlimited
capacity to analyze your data.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.680
There's no dedicated
VMs and no clusters

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.660
for you to worry about,
think about sizing,

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.400
or trying to manage.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.380
Our unique network and
storage infrastructure

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.060
provides incredibly
high throughput,

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.540
making it easy for BigQuery
to analyze exabytes

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.000
of customer data every
day and support streaming

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.710
ingestion of petabytes
of data every day,

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:23.280
as well, all while providing
industry-leading reliability.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.738
And now with the recent
natively-integrated BI engine,

00:02:25.738 --> 00:02:27.780
we're leveraging this
amazing full stack to power

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.022
in memory and real time
analytics, as well.

00:02:31.022 --> 00:02:33.230
I wish I had more time to
walk you through everything

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:35.397
we are working on to provide
an innovation pace that

00:02:35.397 --> 00:02:36.540
supports your business.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:37.957
But unfortunately
I just have time

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:40.618
to call out a few things from
the huge number of capabilities

00:02:40.618 --> 00:02:42.660
that have been released
over the last few months.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:44.330
So whether it's
governance capabilities

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:47.000
like our field-level
encryption, our automatic DLP

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.270
integration with BigQuery which
helps you secure your data

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:53.210
or continue to invest in
advancing in data warehouse,

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:56.403
machine learning with BQML,
or powerful new SQL workload

00:02:56.403 --> 00:02:58.070
management capabilities,
we're committed

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:00.860
to meeting your analytics needs.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:03.270
With that quick recap of
where we've been investing,

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:05.230
I want to pivot to a
deep dive in the areas

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:07.610
investment we're making to
really support your business

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:09.980
building and running
applications in the Cloud,

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:12.410
such as interoperability
support that breaks down

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:16.023
data and application silos,
our migration and management

00:03:16.023 --> 00:03:17.690
support for helping
you get to the Cloud

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:19.750
and save money when
you're here, and finally,

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:22.250
a ton of investments in cracking
that hard problem of really

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:23.990
getting value from
data with real time

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:27.620
and predictive analytics.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.570
As we think about
BigQuery's interoperability,

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:31.610
it's really not like any
other data warehouse.

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:34.940
You get choice of storage,
cloud, tools, engines,

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:37.700
and languages, and we make
that possible to leverage

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:39.650
all these choices with
a consistent security

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:41.450
and governance
framework, so whether it

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:44.090
is our recent launch of Cloud
Spanner Query Federation

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.160
or the launch of
autoscaling serverless Spark

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.680
Integration, which you can
learn more about in the Open

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:50.600
and Integrated Data
Analytics session,

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:52.160
or some of the new capabilities
I'm going to walk you

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.140
through now, you get
to make the choice that

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:57.860
is right for your business
and your data analytics needs.

00:03:57.860 --> 00:03:59.568
Speaking of choice
of cloud, we are

00:03:59.568 --> 00:04:01.610
very excited to announce
the general availability

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:05.330
of BigQuery Omni for Amazon Web
Services and Microsoft Azure.

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:08.000
Most companies have data
across multiple clouds today,

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.620
and that frequently
creates data silos

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:11.690
that make it difficult,
if not even impossible,

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:14.420
for analysts to access
and leverage that data.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:17.570
BigQuery Omni is a flexible
cross cloud analytics solution

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:20.720
that lets you cost effectively
access and securely analyze

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:23.360
data across GCP, AWS, and Azure.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:24.980
With BigQuery Omni,
you can leverage

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:28.670
the familiar BigQuery
UI, API, or standard SQL

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:30.830
to quickly answer
questions and share results

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:32.843
from a single pane of glass.

00:04:32.843 --> 00:04:34.760
With net new capability
that we are announcing

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:37.840
called Cross Cloud Transfer,
you can use standard SQL

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:41.180
to bring the results of
your analysis back to GCP

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.670
with a simple copy statement
and combine that data

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:46.100
and perform advanced analytics
for something like BQML

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:48.470
or aggregate the data from
multiple clouds for Looker

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:50.090
and BI tools.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:52.670
BigQuery Omni is serverless
and connects directly

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:55.430
to your data on AWS or Azure.

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:57.680
You can securely run analytics
on other public cloud

00:04:57.680 --> 00:04:59.360
with a fully managed
infrastructure.

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.370
All compute runs on
BigQuery Omni clusters

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:04.700
in the same AWS or Azure
region as your data storage.

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:06.170
To learn more
about BigQuery Omni

00:05:06.170 --> 00:05:09.050
and see a demo of our new Cross
Cloud Transfer capability,

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:11.450
please visit the Data Analytics
session on multi cloud,

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:15.040
and we encourage you
to come give it a try.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:16.990
In addition to the
recent BigQuery Omni,

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:19.390
happy to announce that we
were making the secure lake

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:22.420
house architecture a reality
across all three clouds.

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:24.730
With the introduction of
authorized external tables,

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:27.040
it is now possible to have
fine-grained governance

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:29.440
and security permissions
on lake house data.

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:32.140
Authorized external tables allow
you to set up a service account

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:35.650
for accessing your data natively
in Google Cloud Storage, S3,

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:37.360
or Azure storage,
so you don't have

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:39.220
to grant privileges
to the raw data

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:41.500
or worry about
physical file layout.

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:43.840
You can use BigQuery's
native policy capabilities

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:46.480
to provide table, column,
or row-level security

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.700
for your data and data
lakes, and those policies

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:50.590
are consistently
enforced, regardless

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:53.050
of whether accessing the
data through SQL, data PROC,

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:55.060
or other engines, like Spark.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:57.880
Net, you no longer have to care
about where your data resides

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:00.130
or what engine you're
using to access it in order

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:03.070
to provide consistent,
secure, auditable, and even

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:05.320
policy-driven
access to your data.

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:10.400
Before today, BigQuery
customers had the ability

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:13.400
to create user-defined
functions in SQL or JavaScript

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:15.230
and run them entirely
within BigQuery,

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:18.020
and while these functions are
performant and fully managed

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:20.660
from within BigQuery,
customers expressed an interest

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:22.660
and desire to
extend BigQuery UVFs

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:24.280
to their own external code.

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:26.030
We had requests from
health care providers

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:27.822
who wanted to bring
their existing security

00:06:27.822 --> 00:06:29.990
platforms to BigQuery,
financial institutions

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:31.490
that wanted to
enrich their BigQuery

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:33.320
data with real-time
stock updates,

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:35.870
and data scientists who wanted
to be able to use Vertex AI

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:37.820
alongside with BQML.

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:40.460
To help these customers extend
BigQuery into other components,

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:43.540
we created BigQuery
External Functions.

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:45.970
BigQuery External Functions
provides a direct integration

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:49.210
with cloud functions,
GCP's serverless execution

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:51.130
environment for
single-purpose functions.

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:52.900
With external
functions in BigQuery,

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:56.110
you'll now be able to write a
function in node, Python, Go,

00:06:56.110 --> 00:07:00.280
Java, .NET, Ruby, or even
PHP and execute it on columns

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:02.680
passed in from
BigQuery SQL queries.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:05.320
BigQuery external functions that
you incorporate BigQuery SQL

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:08.852
functionality with software
outside of BigQuery.

00:07:08.852 --> 00:07:10.810
We've already started
working with key partners

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:13.420
like Protegrity on
using external functions

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:15.640
as a mechanism to merge
BigQuery into their security

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:17.848
platform, which
will help us help

00:07:17.848 --> 00:07:20.140
our mutual customers address
their stringent compliance

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:21.550
controls.

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:24.520
Also happy to announce the
preview of Analytics Hub, which

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:27.310
will help organizations publish,
discover, subscribe, and share

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:29.500
data assets so they can
seek broader insights

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:31.090
and ask bigger questions.

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:33.250
BigQuery has had
cross-organizational data

00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:35.608
sharing in place
capability since inception,

00:07:35.608 --> 00:07:37.900
and we have thousands of
organizations sharing hundreds

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:39.790
of petabytes of data today.

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:42.220
Our Analytics Hub makes
that experience of discovery

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:44.140
and management much easier.

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:45.790
Analytics Hub will
allow publishers

00:07:45.790 --> 00:07:48.700
to create exchanges that
combine unique Google data

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:51.920
sets with commercial industry
data sets and public data sets.

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:53.920
Publishers will be able
to curate data exchanges

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:55.900
internally and
externally, and they'll

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:58.630
be able to view aggregated
usage metrics on how popular

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:00.340
their exchanges are.

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:02.110
With other interoperability
capabilities

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:04.027
I mentioned, it will
soon be possible to share

00:08:04.027 --> 00:08:06.250
data in the Analytics Hub
in place from your data

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:08.620
to data lake, as well.

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:10.270
In addition to the
unique Google data

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:12.220
that we are working to
bring to Analytics Hub,

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:14.470
such as the recent release
of Google Trends data,

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:18.160
which enables discovery of top
search terms across locations,

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:20.380
we're also partnering
with Crux Informatics

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:23.770
to accelerate access to
data on Analytics Hub.

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:27.280
Data providers across finance,
geospatial, retail, and more

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:28.930
are all bringing
their data to BigQuery

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:30.850
and delivering it
through Analytics Hub.

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:32.320
We will announce
more partnerships

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:35.230
as we move closer to general
availability of Analytics Hub

00:08:35.230 --> 00:08:37.240
next year.

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:40.030
Deciding to move to the
Cloud is a big commitment,

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:42.713
both in tech, processes,
and change management.

00:08:42.713 --> 00:08:45.130
And knowing that it is the
right choice for your business,

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:46.960
both for the
capabilities it unlocks

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:50.440
but also for the overall
TCO, is equally important.

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:51.940
Happy to highlight
some capabilities

00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:54.610
we're invested in that make
all aspects of moving your data

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:59.690
warehouse to the Cloud and to
BigQuery on GCP a good bet.

00:08:59.690 --> 00:09:01.670
I'm excited to introduce
the BigQuery migration

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:05.090
service, a comprehensive
solution for migrating data

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:09.560
to BigQuery which will enable
fast and low-risk migrations.

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:11.330
The BigQuery migration
service speeds up

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:14.090
Teradata to BigQuery migrations
with predictable tooling

00:09:14.090 --> 00:09:15.710
for customers and
partners, which

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:19.490
cover migration planning, data
transfer, automatic SQL script

00:09:19.490 --> 00:09:21.800
conversion, and
data verification.

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:25.790
Support for additional data
warehouses is also coming soon.

00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:28.430
One of the hardest pieces of
any data warehouse migration

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:31.070
is modernizing legacy business
logic like SQL queries,

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:33.320
scripts, and stored procedures.

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:35.090
The BigQuery migration
service provides

00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:37.880
fast, semantically-correct and
human-readable translations

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:40.760
of legacy objects with
no ongoing dependencies.

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:43.230
It supports a broad range
of Teradata artifacts,

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:46.130
including DML, DDL, and
BTEQ, and translations

00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:48.590
can be run in batch
mode or ad hoc directly

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:50.690
from the BigQuery SQL Workspace.

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:52.790
Customers like Walmart
and MercadoLibre

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:55.323
have used it to successfully
translate millions of queries,

00:09:55.323 --> 00:09:56.990
and we encourage you
to come give a try.

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:02.970
When doing analytics at scale,
understanding what is happening

00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:05.640
and being able to take action
in real time is critical,

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:08.220
and many of our customers also
desire capacity management

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:11.190
capabilities to optimize
their BQ environments.

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:13.770
We're happy to launch a new
BigQuery Administration Hub

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:15.960
experience with new
features such as research

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:18.060
charts and the slot estimator.

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:19.703
The capabilities
help our admin users

00:10:19.703 --> 00:10:21.870
to understand and manage
their BigQuery environments

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:23.640
like never before.

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:25.380
Research charts
provide a native,

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:27.810
out-of-the-box experience
for real-time monitoring

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:30.295
and troubleshooting of
your BigQuery environments.

00:10:30.295 --> 00:10:31.920
These charts make it
easy to understand

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:34.410
your historical patterns
across slot consumption, job

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:36.510
concurrency, and
job performance,

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:38.250
allowing you to take
actions to ensure

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:40.740
your BigQuery environment
continues to run smoothly.

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:43.170
This is now generally available
for all customers that

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:45.680
purchase slot reservations.

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:47.600
Many customers with
pre-purchase capacity

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:50.360
also want to understand,
should I buy more?

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:51.650
Do I have too many slots?

00:10:51.650 --> 00:10:54.080
Will additional slots
affect my job performance?

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:56.840
The slot estimator is an
interactive capacity management

00:10:56.840 --> 00:10:59.090
tool that helps administrators
estimate and optimize

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:02.030
their BigQuery capacity
based on jobs performance.

00:11:02.030 --> 00:11:04.310
It looks at historical usage
and helps customers make

00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:06.300
capacity planning decisions.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:08.570
It also provides estimates
on price for performance

00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:10.310
when adding or reducing slots.

00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:13.100
This is now available for public
preview for all reservation

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:16.160
customers.

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:17.600
In addition to
the Admin tooling,

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:20.240
sometimes customers have one
off or spiking workloads,

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:22.610
and flex slots can
be a great solution.

00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:25.280
In fact, many customers have
been leveraging flex slots

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:27.500
to scale up or down their
environments via simple SQL

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:28.370
statements.

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:30.530
This is great for
one-off batch workloads,

00:11:30.530 --> 00:11:33.447
but does require you to estimate
and manage your capacity.

00:11:33.447 --> 00:11:35.780
We're now happy to announce
the preview of the Flex Slot

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:36.800
Autoscaler.

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:38.360
Autoscaling enables
customers to take

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:41.450
full advantage of BigQuery's
elastic serverless architecture

00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:44.390
while maintaining
controls over cost.

00:11:44.390 --> 00:11:46.280
With autoscaling, you
simply set the size

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:48.280
of the environment you're
willing to scale up to

00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:51.200
and then BigQuery looks at
your current query load.

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:53.673
And if more budget is
available and more capacity

00:11:53.673 --> 00:11:55.340
would accelerate your
queries in flight,

00:11:55.340 --> 00:11:57.140
it scales up your
environment on the fly

00:11:57.140 --> 00:11:58.580
in very small increments.

00:11:58.580 --> 00:12:01.190
By adjusting the size of your
environment every few seconds,

00:12:01.190 --> 00:12:04.400
you get maximum performance
without any wasted capacity

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:05.852
up to your budget.

00:12:05.852 --> 00:12:07.310
Customers like Snap
have been using

00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:09.727
this to provide phenomenal
performance for their analysts,

00:12:09.727 --> 00:12:12.170
and we've had customers
scale up tens of thousands

00:12:12.170 --> 00:12:14.120
of slots for incredible
performance on really

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:16.998
large workloads.

00:12:16.998 --> 00:12:18.540
In addition to the
great capabilities

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:20.670
we've announced for managing
your query resources,

00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:22.170
we're also adding
additional support

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:23.580
for managing your storage.

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:25.860
Snapshots, currently
in Preview, provide

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:28.080
read-only, point-in-time
versions of tables

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:30.000
without actually having
to copy the base data

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:31.800
and incur duplicate costs.

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:34.117
They can be really useful
for logical backups,

00:12:34.117 --> 00:12:36.450
saving the state of something
like a financial dashboard

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:39.690
to keep points in time,
and many other use cases.

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:43.740
Clones, coming soon, provide
modifiable versions of tables

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:45.705
without having to copy
the base data, as well.

00:12:45.705 --> 00:12:47.580
This makes it possible
for you to test things

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:49.788
like schema evolution, where
you're modifying columns

00:12:49.788 --> 00:12:52.470
for the rollout of a new
version of your application

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:54.360
or to set up dev test
environments that work

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:57.030
end-to-end so you can run
crews on a large amount of data

00:12:57.030 --> 00:12:59.070
without incurring the
cost of duplicate data

00:12:59.070 --> 00:13:00.811
just for a test environment.

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:05.230
Once you're in the
Cloud, it's time

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:07.313
to really leverage the
capabilities that have been

00:13:07.313 --> 00:13:09.680
unlocked that will enable
you to drive your business

00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:11.760
and really drive
value from that data.

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:13.510
Happy to highlight
some of the investments

00:13:13.510 --> 00:13:16.400
across the full stack, from
high throughput investment to BI

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:21.080
in tooling and ML that will
help you unlock value from data.

00:13:21.080 --> 00:13:23.840
For customers with needs for
real-time data analytics,

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:27.080
the new BigQuery Write API
is now generally available.

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:29.588
This new Write API underscores
our engineering innovation

00:13:29.588 --> 00:13:31.130
to bring industry-leading
performance

00:13:31.130 --> 00:13:32.920
for streaming ingestion.

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:34.900
With over 1 gigabyte
per second of ingestion

00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:36.790
throughput enabled by
default and the ability

00:13:36.790 --> 00:13:40.090
to scale to 100 times or more
that, the Write API allows

00:13:40.090 --> 00:13:42.490
you to unify streaming
and batch ingestion

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:45.040
and offers exact once
delivery semantics.

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:46.990
It also supports
stream-level transactions

00:13:46.990 --> 00:13:49.610
and automatic detection
of target schema changes.

00:13:49.610 --> 00:13:51.610
These features, combined
with native integration

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:53.170
with the data flow,
will allow you

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:56.312
to accelerate your real-time
analytics workloads.

00:13:56.312 --> 00:13:57.770
I'm also happy to
tell you that all

00:13:57.770 --> 00:14:01.240
of this net new value over the
previous Streaming API, yet

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:03.490
is priced at 50% cheaper
than the current offerings.

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:05.965
So we encourage you to move
to the new BigQuery Write API.

00:14:08.850 --> 00:14:10.680
BigQuery is a powerful
analytics engine

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:13.290
that provides great performance
on calculations, aggregations

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:14.910
for data at any scale.

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:16.740
However, the way
customers use BigQuery

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:20.628
has expanded from traditional
data warehouses or data lakes,

00:14:20.628 --> 00:14:22.920
and today there are many
applications built on BigQuery

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:24.420
that may not want
to crunch numbers,

00:14:24.420 --> 00:14:27.030
but simply return a
handful of rows associated

00:14:27.030 --> 00:14:30.180
with a specific data point,
like a name or an IP address.

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:32.820
Customers expressed that when
finding these specific data

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:35.010
points in terabytes
or petabytes of data,

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:37.680
it was slower, maybe
even more cost expensive,

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:40.350
than the applications
were designed for.

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:42.690
This is because BigQuery
needs to do a full table scan

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:45.180
and read all of it just to
find the rows associated

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:46.620
with the unique element.

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:48.870
To help customers build these
operational applications

00:14:48.870 --> 00:14:51.120
on top of BigQuery, we
are announcing new search

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:52.620
capabilities in BigQuery.

00:14:52.620 --> 00:14:55.020
Customers can now
create Search Indexes

00:14:55.020 --> 00:14:57.270
for a table that accelerates
the speed at which you

00:14:57.270 --> 00:15:00.150
can identify the rows that
contain specific text.

00:15:00.150 --> 00:15:02.460
You can use BigQuery Search
Indexes and Preview today

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:04.710
if you have scenarios that
would benefit from speed up

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:05.820
of point lookups.

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:07.590
Or another way,
Search Indexes are

00:15:07.590 --> 00:15:10.170
useful when you need to
find a needle in a haystack.

00:15:10.170 --> 00:15:12.330
Search Indexes can be used
across multiple columns

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:12.830
at once.

00:15:12.830 --> 00:15:14.955
So even if you don't know
exactly where the data is

00:15:14.955 --> 00:15:16.230
stored, it can still be found.

00:15:16.230 --> 00:15:18.870
It's also fully integrated
with our native JSON data type,

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:20.563
also in Preview,
meaning that you

00:15:20.563 --> 00:15:22.230
can search for fields
and values in data

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:25.630
that is unstructured or has a
constantly changing structure.

00:15:25.630 --> 00:15:27.923
These search indexes also
back our new log analytics

00:15:27.923 --> 00:15:29.590
application that was
recently announced,

00:15:29.590 --> 00:15:32.260
Cloud Logging, which helps use
BigQuery to better understand

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:35.002
your telemetry and data.

00:15:35.002 --> 00:15:36.960
Like the rest of BigQuery,
these search indexes

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:38.640
are fully managed
and serverless.

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:40.380
Google has all the
resource provisioning,

00:15:40.380 --> 00:15:43.260
tokenizes the data, and loads
the index behind the scenes.

00:15:43.260 --> 00:15:45.180
So once you create a
Search Index on a table,

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:47.580
you can start streaming data
in and know that your search

00:15:47.580 --> 00:15:49.470
indexes will
automatically be refreshed

00:15:49.470 --> 00:15:51.048
without any management overhead.

00:15:51.048 --> 00:15:52.590
We've been working
with our customers

00:15:52.590 --> 00:15:54.215
on this feature, who
started testing it

00:15:54.215 --> 00:15:55.770
for a variety of
different use cases.

00:15:55.770 --> 00:15:57.323
For example, a
major retailer found

00:15:57.323 --> 00:15:58.740
that they are
improving dashboards

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:00.510
that have highly
selective inventory items

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:02.236
that they need to
slice and dice.

00:16:02.236 --> 00:16:04.236
We've also worked for
several companies in the U

00:16:04.236 --> 00:16:06.990
who are hoping use Search
Indexes to dramatically reduce

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:09.810
costs for their GDPR
processes, since they're now

00:16:09.810 --> 00:16:14.990
able to touch only the rows that
contain specific individuals,

00:16:14.990 --> 00:16:16.950
no longer having to
query the full data set

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:18.810
for each GDPR request.

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:21.060
And of course, we found a
myriad of other improvements

00:16:21.060 --> 00:16:22.853
for log analytics
applications that

00:16:22.853 --> 00:16:25.020
can now use BigQuery for
efficient lookups of things

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:27.735
like IP addresses, error
codes, emails, and URLs.

00:16:30.027 --> 00:16:31.860
We were one of the first
to bring integrated

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:34.740
machine learning into the data
warehouse with BigQuery ML,

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:36.490
and we continue to see
tremendous adoption

00:16:36.490 --> 00:16:38.615
and customer innovation
because of the ease of use,

00:16:38.615 --> 00:16:41.010
ability to unlock ML with
only a few lines of SQL,

00:16:41.010 --> 00:16:42.990
and no infrastructure
management.

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:45.390
In addition to continued
investment in additional models

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:48.210
and capabilities, like
XG Boost, AutoML Tables,

00:16:48.210 --> 00:16:50.040
and Hyperparameter
Tuning, I wanted

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:52.500
to highlight our work
around a couple of areas.

00:16:52.500 --> 00:16:55.080
First off, Explainable AI.

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:57.180
Explainable AI helps you
understand the results

00:16:57.180 --> 00:16:59.013
that your predictive
machine learning models

00:16:59.013 --> 00:17:02.610
generate by defining how
much each feature contributes

00:17:02.610 --> 00:17:04.050
to the predicted results.

00:17:04.050 --> 00:17:06.200
This is referred to as
feature attribution.

00:17:06.200 --> 00:17:08.700
This information can be used
to verify the model as behaving

00:17:08.700 --> 00:17:11.323
as expected, to recognize
bias in your models,

00:17:11.323 --> 00:17:12.990
and to inform ways
to improve your model

00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:14.190
and your training data.

00:17:14.190 --> 00:17:17.520
We support both local and
global explainability.

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:20.369
We're also investing in
integrating BQML and Vertex AI

00:17:20.369 --> 00:17:24.299
closer together so you can
leverage the best of both

00:17:24.299 --> 00:17:25.859
for ML ops deployments.

00:17:25.859 --> 00:17:28.470
For example, BQML models
will be registered in Vertex

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:31.510
and they can be used to manage
versions and compare there.

00:17:31.510 --> 00:17:33.660
We'll also be adding
support in Vertex Pipelines.

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:35.340
So all BQML training
and prediction

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:37.720
will be possible through
Vertex pipeline components.

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:40.200
So this user can continue to
use their favorite notebook

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:42.540
and use BQML pipeline
components to vote, training,

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:44.070
and prediction.

00:17:44.070 --> 00:17:46.110
Overall, in partnership
with the Vertex AI team,

00:17:46.110 --> 00:17:48.720
we're committed to delivering
a powerful and fully integrated

00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:53.140
ML solution with
great ease of use.

00:17:53.140 --> 00:17:55.690
Finally, happy to announce
the launch of BigQuery's

00:17:55.690 --> 00:17:58.420
BI Engine, our solution for
enabling data and business

00:17:58.420 --> 00:18:01.180
analysts to perform interactive
analytics in real-time

00:18:01.180 --> 00:18:03.160
and with high concurrency.

00:18:03.160 --> 00:18:04.540
BI Engine is not a cache.

00:18:04.540 --> 00:18:07.030
It is a fully-distributed,
highly-available, in-memory

00:18:07.030 --> 00:18:09.430
database Engine, which
is natively incorporated

00:18:09.430 --> 00:18:11.110
into standard BigQuery APIs.

00:18:11.110 --> 00:18:13.600
It enables subsequent
queries on large data

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:16.390
sets with high concurrency
and can eliminate the need

00:18:16.390 --> 00:18:18.820
to manage BI servers
or ETL pipelines,

00:18:18.820 --> 00:18:21.550
and it natively works with SQL
and BI tools such as Looker,

00:18:21.550 --> 00:18:24.100
Tableau, Power BI, et cetera.

00:18:24.100 --> 00:18:27.460
For GA, we added support for
pinning preferred tables and up

00:18:27.460 --> 00:18:30.370
to one terabyte of
memory per reservation

00:18:30.370 --> 00:18:33.518
and additional joint operations
and support for streaming.

00:18:33.518 --> 00:18:35.560
This means that in
combination with the new Write

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:37.480
API for high
throughput streaming,

00:18:37.480 --> 00:18:40.000
materialized views which have
also been recently released,

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:42.730
and BI Engine for acceleration
of interactive analytics,

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:44.350
these technologies
all work together

00:18:44.350 --> 00:18:47.950
to make the vision of always
fresh, always fast analytics

00:18:47.950 --> 00:18:51.570
a reality at any scale.

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:53.100
That was quite
the whirlwind tour

00:18:53.100 --> 00:18:54.528
of what's new with BigQuery.

00:18:54.528 --> 00:18:56.070
Thank you for joining
us today, and I

00:18:56.070 --> 00:18:57.820
hope you can see our
commitment to provide

00:18:57.820 --> 00:18:59.580
powerful analytic
solutions that will

00:18:59.580 --> 00:19:01.830
help drive your businesses
forward and help you unlock

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:02.615
data.

00:19:02.615 --> 00:19:03.990
If you'd like to
learn more, here

00:19:03.990 --> 00:19:05.573
are some additional
sessions I suggest

00:19:05.573 --> 00:19:08.310
you take a look at to see
the power of Omni, Serverless

00:19:08.310 --> 00:19:13.290
Spark, Streaming, Log Analytics,
and Secure Data Classification

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:14.460
with Google Cloud.

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:16.950
Thanks again for joining me
to learn more about BigQuery,

00:19:16.950 --> 00:19:19.980
and I hope we can help you
drive your business and data

00:19:19.980 --> 00:19:21.580
analytics for your
company forward.

00:19:21.580 --> 00:19:23.330
Thank you.

